=== Clock Synthesis ===

[[File:MX8350-Table.png|thumb|alt=MX8350 functional description and frequency table.|MX8350 functional description and frequency table. Image from: [http://www.datasheet.hk/view_download.php?id=1145313&file=0069%5Cmx8350_566191.pdf IC-ON-LINE - Macronix MX8350 datasheet mirror]]]
[[File:MX8330 video maths.png|thumb|alt=MX8330MC description. FSEL (frequency select) logic and Revision E notice|MX8330 description. FSEL (frequency select) logic and Revision E notice. Image from: [https://wiki.console5.com/tw/images/e/e3/MX8330.pdf Console5 - Macronix MX8330MC datasheet mirror]]]

The clock synthesizer (Macronix MX8330MC / MX9911MC / MX8350) derives the necessary clocks from the X1 crystal input:

* '''FSC''': Chrominance subcarrier = X1 ÷ 4
* '''VCLK''': Video clock = X1 × multiplier ÷ 5

'''FSEL (NTSC/!PAL)''':<ref>This pin is marked FSEL on the MX8330MC and MX9911MC datasheets; the equivalent pin on the MX8350 is referred to as '''NTSC/!PAL'''.</ref> Input pin; tied high or low. Selects the VCLK multiplier:

{| class="wikitable" style="text-align: center;"
! Standard !! FSEL Pin State !! Multiplier
|-
| '''NTSC, MPAL''' || High || 17×
|-
| '''PAL''' || Low || 14×
|}

Video clock frequency is calculated as:

<math>VCLK = \frac{X1 \times \text{Multiplier}}{5}</math>

{| class="wikitable" style="text-align: center;"
! Standard !! FSC (<math>f_{SC}</math>) !! Crystal 1 (X1) !! FSEL (NTSC/!PAL) !! Multiplier !! Divider !! VCLK
|-
| '''NTSC''' || 3.57954545 MHz || 14.31818182 MHz || High || 17 || 5 || 48.68181818 MHz
|-
| '''PAL''' || 4.43361875 MHz || 17.734475 MHz || Low || 14 || 5 || 49.65653 MHz
|-
| '''MPAL''' || 3.57561189 MHz || 14.30244755 MHz || High || 17 || 5 || 48.62832167 MHz
|}

The [[Audio Interface]] also uses this clock.

== Crystal 2 (X2) ==

=== Rambus Clock Path (RCLK) ===

A second quartz crystal resonator (X2) provides the reference for the Rambus subsystem. This crystal frequency is multiplied by a fixed factor of 17 to produce the high-speed Rambus clock domain.

For a nominal X2 frequency of 14.705882 MHz, the synthesizer generates:

* '''RCLK = 250 MHz''' (X2 × 17)

Unlike the video clock path (X1), this clock is distributed using Rambus Signaling Logic (RSL), a low-voltage swing interface optimized for high-speed operation. The synthesizer drives this signal via the '''FSO''' output pin, rather than the TTL-compatible '''FSO/5''' used in VCLK generation<ref>FSC output is also TTL-compatible</ref>.

RCLK is routed directly to the RDRAM devices and into the Rambus ASIC Clock (RAC) domain within the RCP. From this point, the clock is synchronously divided to produce the internal system timing hierarchy, including the memory clock (MClock), system interface (SI), and downstream peripheral clocks.

The CPU clock originates from the Rambus clock domain via MClock, and is further scaled by programmable divisors (DivMode registers).

=== X2 Clock Distribution ===

{| class="wikitable" style="text-align:center;"
|-
! Stage !! Clock !! Derivation !! Exact (MHz) !! MHz !! Notes
|-
| 1 || X2 || — || <math>\frac{250}{17}</math> || 14.7058823529 || Secondary crystal dedicated to Rambus timing
|-
| 2 || RCLK || X2 × 17 || <math>250</math> || 250 || Generated by synthesizer; driven via RSL (FSO)
|-
| 3 || MClock (RAC) || RCLK ÷ 4 || <math>\frac{250}{4}</math> || 62.5 || Internal RCP memory/control domain
|-
| 4 || CPU || MClock × <math>\frac{3}{2}</math> || <math>\frac{750}{8}</math> || 93.75<ref>CPU clock is software-configurable via DivMode registers. 93.75 MHz is the nominal operating frequency.</ref> || Central Processing Unit
|-
| 5 || SI || MClock ÷ 4 || <math>\frac{250}{16}</math> || 15.625 || System interface and peripheral timing base
|-
| 6 || Cartridge / PIF || SI ÷ 8 || <math>\frac{250}{128}</math> || 1.953125 || Cartridge bus and PIF clock domain
|}

=== Notes ===

* The use of a dedicated crystal for the Rambus domain isolates high-speed memory timing from the video clock domain (X1), minimizing jitter coupling between subsystems.
* The RSL-driven FSO output is electrically distinct from TTL outputs and is required to meet Rambus signaling constraints at 250 MHz.
* The division chain (÷4 → ×3/2 → ÷4 → ÷8) establishes a fully synchronous hierarchy of sub-clocks across the RCP, CPU, and peripheral interfaces.
* Although 93.75 MHz is the nominal CPU frequency, the actual operating frequency depends on DivMode configuration and may vary accordingly.

